## ABSTRACT
Zeolite-metal organic framework nanocomposite catalyst is developed for the synthesis of 2- ((5-(hydroxymethyl)furan-2-yl)methylene)malononitrile from sucrose/fructose/glucose via one-pot cascade protocol. To accomplish this objective, zeolite Beta and Zr based metalorganic frameworks such as UiO-66 and UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ are investigated for the independent steps, i.e. the conversion of sucrose to HMF and the Knoevenagel condensation of HMF and malononitrile. Beta zeolite exhibits the best activity in the sucrose to HMF conversion, whereas UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ exhibits the best activity in the Knoevenagel condensation. Therefore, zeolite Beta and UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ are integrated to develop a highly sustainable and efficient multifunctional catalyst for the conversion of sucrose to 2-((5-(hydroxymethyl)furan-2- yl)methylene)malononitrile via one-pot cascade reaction. The composite catalyst contains the optimum acidity and basicity for the cascade reaction. The presence of both the frameworks in the composite material is confirmed by using various physico-chemical characterization techniques. Further, the integration of both the active frameworks enhances the chemical stability and recyclability of the composite catalyst in the cascade reaction. This study demonstrates a unique approach to develop a synthesis strategy for the integration of inorganic zeolite and MOFs to achieve the desired activity, selectivity, and stability of the developed catalyst.
Keywords: Metal-organic framework, zeolite, composite material, biomass transformation, HMF, and HMF derivatives
## 1. Introduction
Commodity chemicals, food, and pharmaceutical industries are heavily depending on the fossil-fuel based feedstock. The depletion in fossil fuel reserves and the escalating nature of environmental pollution have persuaded chemists to develop alternative feedstocks to tackle these challenges. Alternative carbon sources can be produced by the growth of biomass and by the effective conversion of biomass to useful chemicals [1-3]. Biomass, cultivated in different countries, has variable compositions of lignin, cellulose, and hemicelluloses. It is possible to convert all the three components into alternative transportation fuel or fuel additives [4-6]. However, commodity chemicals, polymer industry, food, and pharmaceutical industries find interest in the conversion of cellulose to monosaccharide and disaccharide, followed by their conversion to 5- hydroxymethylfurfural and its derivative [7-17].
Synthesis of 5-hydroxymethylfurfural (HMF) from monosaccharide/disaccharide and direct conversion of monosaccharide/disaccharide to HMF derived chemicals require multifunctional catalyst [18-34]. Such multi-functional catalysts must have Brönsted acidity and Lewis acidity of suitable strength apart from the required functionality for the desired chemical transformation of HMF [31-34]. Since the conversion of monosaccharide/disaccharide requires somewhat stringent reaction condition, the catalyst should be stable. When stable catalysts are considered, zeolites, metal oxides, and silica derived catalysts are of significant importance. The recent advancement in their (zeolites, metal oxides, and silica) synthesis and properties, it is possible to achieve multi-functional catalysts based on these materials [35-39].
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are another class of materials, which are receiving significant attention in recent times as catalysts [40-47]. Isolated metal centers and organic linkers provide an opportunity to create multi-functional activity in the MOFs. Functional sites in the MOFs can be introduced either by selecting functional group bearing organic linkers or by the postfunctionalization of organic and heterocyclic skeleton of MOFs [48-51]. Furthermore, metal centers present in MOF can also act as sites for the coordination of desired functionality for catalytic reactions [52-56]. In recent times, the emphasis has been laid on the development of MOFs for the conversion of carbohydrate to HMF [57-59]. Literature reports suggest that amine-functionalized MOFs are more suitable for the Knoevenagel condensation [60-63]. The conversion of carbohydrates to HMF and HMF-derivatives is generally catalyzed in a highly polar medium such as water, DMSO, DMF, and ethanol. Mostly, MOFs synthesis takes place in a similar reaction medium (as mentioned above), and hence, leaching of MOFs during carbohydrate to HMF conversion process poses a major obstacle. Thus, it is important to develop highly stable MOFs based catalysts. In the recent times, efforts have been made to integrate MOFs with other inorganic materials such as zeolites and silica to provide stability to the MOFs so that the catalyst can be easily recycled and reused [63-67]. The integration of zeolites (having Brönsted and Lewis acidity) and MOFs would be interesting to develop multi-functional catalysts for carbohydrate transformations [63-65].
Herein, highly stable, multi-functional UiO-66 and UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ MOFs were prepared. H-Beta zeolite was integrated with $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ to develop a highly sustainable multifunctional catalyst. The synthesized Beta-UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ catalyst exhibited excellent activity, stability, and recyclability for the one-pot conversion of sucrose to 2-((5- (hydroxymethyl)furan-2-yl)methylene)malononitrile.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1 Synthesis of Beta $(10\%)$ -UiO-66-NH2
For the synthesis of Beta $(10\%)$ -UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ , the surface of the pre-activated Beta zeolite (473 K for $8\mathrm{~h~}$ ) was first modified with terminal carboxylic acid by following the reported procedure.52 Succinic anhydride $(6~\mathrm{g})$ was dissolved in N, $N.$ -dimethylformamide (DMF, 80 mL), followed by the addition of (3-aminopropyl) triethoxysilane (APTES, $14~\mathrm{ml}$ ) and stirring the mixture at ambient condition for $30\mathrm{min}$ . Finally, $2.0\ \mathrm{g}$ of Beta was added, and the resultant mixture was stirred at ambient temperature for $24\mathrm{h}$ in the nitrogen atmosphere. The resultant solid product was centrifuged and washed with ethanol several times to remove the excess of the succinic acid/APTES. The final functionalized zeolite was dried in a vacuum oven at $373\mathrm{K}$ for $12\mathrm{h}$ to obtain the Beta- ${\mathrm{.C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ -NHCO- ${\mathrm{.C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}$ -COOH.
2-amino-terephthalic acid $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC-NH}_{2}\mathrm{)}$ $(1.94~\mathrm{g},10.7~\mathrm{mmol})$ was added to a solution containing $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (38.4 ml) and acetic acid ${\bf\zeta}^{9.2\ m l}$ , 160.1 mmol) in a round bottom flask, which was fitted with a reflux condenser. Then, $\mathrm{ZrOCl_{2}.8H_{2}O\ (3.44\mathrm{g},10.7\mathrm{mmol)}}$ was added slowly to the above reaction mixture with continuous stirring, followed by the addition of Beta- ${\mathrm{.C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ - NHCO- ${\cdot}{\mathrm{C}}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ -COOH $(0.54~\mathrm{g})$ and the dispersion of reaction mixture for $20~\mathrm{min}$ using an ultrasonic bath. The reaction mixture was heated at $373\mathrm{~K~}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The yellow solid was filtered, washed with $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and ethanol, followed by drying in a vacuum oven for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ at 373 K. The un-reacted organic linkers were removed by stirring the solid overnight in DMF (50 mL) at ambient temperature. The solid was filtered and dried in a vacuum oven at $373\mathrm{~K~}$ for
$12\mathrm{h}$ .  Moreover, solid was also treated with methanol at room temperature for $12\mathrm{{h}}$ to exchange the coordinated solvent from the framework. The amount of Beta was also varied during this study and Beta( $12.5\%)$ -UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , Beta $(5\%)$ -UiO-66-NH2 and Beta $(2.5\%)$ -UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ were prepared by the addition of $0.67\mathrm{g},0.27\mathrm{g}$ , and $0.13\mathrm{g}$ of Beta- $\mathrm{.C_{3}H_{6}\mathrm{-NHCO-C_{2}H_{4}}}$ -COOH, respectively, during the synthesis of composite materials. Scheme 1a presents the schematic presentation for the synthesis of Beta $(10\%)$ -UiO-66-NH2. Details for the synthesis of UiO-66 and $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\-}{\-}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ are provided in supporting information (SI).
