## 3. Results and discussion
In this study, first UiO-66 and UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ were prepared by using terephthalic acid and 2- amino-terephthalic acid, respectively, as linkers. Further, zeolite Beta-MOF composite was prepared by employing a simple synthesis strategy involving the reaction of surface-functionalized Beta with zirconium MOF precursors. Initially, required the amount of functionalized Beta was treated with zirconium source $\mathrm{(ZrOCl_{2}.8H_{2}O)}$ ) and 2-amino-terephthalic acid to get Beta integrated MOF composite, and the obtained material is designated as Beta $(\mathbf{{x\%}})$ -UiO-66-NH2 (Where $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{x}}=$ 2.5 to 12.5). Materials with different mass loading of Beta in the range of $2.5~\mathrm{wt\%}$ to $12.5~\mathrm{wt\%}$ were prepared. The detailed characterizations are provided below.
## 3.1. Physico-chemical characterization
The crystalline nature of MOFs and composite materials was investigated by powder XRD (Fig. 1 and Fig. S1). The well-resolved powder XRD patterns of UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ and Beta (Fig. 1) are analogous to the XRD patterns of the reported UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and BEA framework, respectively [68, 69]. For comparative study, UiO-66 was prepared and the XRD pattern of UiO-66 matches well with the reported XRD pattern of UiO-66 (Fig. S1) [69, 64]. The XRD pattern of $\mathrm{Beta}(10\%)–\mathrm{UiO}–66–\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ shows the overlapping reflections of Beta with the highly intense UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ framework reflections. The composite material displays a characteristic peak of Beta at $2\uptheta=22.63^{\circ}$ which reflects the preservation of both the frameworks after the integration of Beta zeolite with UiO-66- $\left.\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right.$ in the composite material (Fig. 1).
The nitrogen sorption measurements were carried out to determine the physical characteristics of the materials (Fig. S2). The $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption profile of the Beta zeolite exhibits type II isotherm (Fig. S2a). Almost a flat adsorption is observed in the range of $0{\cdot}0.6\ (\mathfrak{p}/\mathfrak{p}_{0})$ . Only a low increase in the adsorption volume above 0.6 $\left(\mathrm{P/P_{0}}\right)$ for Beta zeolite corresponds to the condensation of ${\bf N}_{2}$ in inter-crystalline void space. The volume increase above $\mathrm{P/P}_{0}=0.9$ $\left(\mathrm{P/P_{0}}\right)$ indicates the presence of inter-particle porosity. The $\Nu_{2}$ -adsorption profiles for UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and Beta( $10\%$ -UiO-66-NH2 exhibit type II isotherm (Fig. S2a). Almost a flat adsorption is observed in the range of $0{-}0.85\ \mathrm{(p/p_{0})}$ . A low increase in the adsorption volume is observed above 0.85 $\left(\mathrm{P/P_{0}}\right)$ which corresponds to the condensation of $\Nu_{2}$ in inter-crystalline void space. Somewhat lower adsorption volume is observed for Beta( $10\%$ -UiO-66-NH2 as compared to UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ which shows the reduction in the surface area of the nanocomposite (Table 1 summarizes the physical characteristics of the materials). A type IV isotherm is obtained for UiO-66 which shows the adsorption in $0{-}0.05\ (\mathrm{p/p_{0}})$ for micropores, a gradual increase in the adsorption in the range of $0.1\mathrm{-0.7\(p/p_{0})}$ for inter-crystalline void space, and steep increase in the adsorption with HI hysteresis in the range of 0.7-0.95 $\left(\mathrm{P/P_{0}}\right)$ for mesopores present in the material (Fig. S2b). The details of the textural properties of these materials are presented in Table 1.
The surface morphology of the parent MOFs and the composite catalyst was investigated using scanning electron microscopy (SEM) (Fig. 2 and Fig. S3). UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ exhibits a uniform small spherical morphology with particle sizes in the range of $100{-}200~\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 2a). The SEM image of Beta exhibits aggregated nanocrystals morphology (Fig. 2b). The morphology of Beta- ${\mathrm{.C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ -NHCO- $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ -COOH is very similar to that of parent Beta, except for some aggregation in the morphology (Fig. 2c). Morphology of Beta $(10\%)$ -UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ is very similar to that of UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}/\mathrm{l}$ Beta which shows that the carboxylate groups present in Beta- ${\mathrm{.C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ -NHCO- ${\cdot}{\mathrm{C}}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ -COOH interact with $Z\mathbf{r}$ metals and participate in the crystallization process and produced materials with the morphology similar to that of $\mathrm{Beta/UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (Fig. 2d). The energy dispersive X-ray (EDX) analysis was investigated to analyze the elemental composition of Beta, UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and their composite material (Fig. S4). Zr, O, C and N elements are observed in the EDX spectrum of UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . Moreover, the presence of additional peaks corresponding to Si and Al elements in the EDX spectrum of Beta $(10\%)$ -UiO$66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ is clearly visible which confirms the integration of Beta and UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ frameworks in the composite material. Further elemental mapping verified the homogenous distribution of all the elements (Zr, C, N, O, Si and Al elements) in the composite material (Fig. S5). Very large irregular crystal morphology is observed for UiO-66 (Fig. S3).
FE-SEM images were recorded for the composite material Beta $(10\%)$ -UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ (Fig. 2 e-f). The composite material exhibits spherical morphology with varied sizes. No clear distinction between Beta and UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ nanocrystals is observed from the FESEM images. It is presumed that smaller aggregates correspond to the Beta zeolite whereas the larger aggregated domains correspond to UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ . To get the different domains of two different frameworks in the composite material, images were recorded with transmission electron microscopy (TEM) (Fig. 3 and Fig. S6). Beta( $10\%$ )-UiO-66-NH2 exhibits two distinguished morphologies of different sizes. Small aggregated zeolite Beta nanocrystals (with particle sizes $10{-}20~\mathrm{nm}$ ) are highly integrated with large spherical morphology with particle sizes of 60-80 nm. Further EDAX spectrum recorded during TEM investigation confirms the presence of all the elements (Zr, C, N, O, Si and Al elements) in the composite material (Fig. S6).
The presence of both the frameworks in the composite material was investigated by the Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy (Fig. 4a and Fig. S7a). The peak at $1571~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ is attributed to asymmetric stretching, whereas the peaks at 1424 and $1387\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ correspond to symmetric stretching vibrations of carboxylate linker present in the UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ (Fig. 4a). The characteristic peaks at 1627 and $1255\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ correspond to the C-N stretching of aromatic amine and N-H bending vibration, respectively. The internal and external asymmetric stretching vibrations of O–T–O in the Beta zeolite are observed at 1061 and $1208\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively. The absorption at 424 and $787~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ corresponds to the $_{\mathrm{T-O}}$ bending mode and O–T–O external symmetric stretching, respectively. The additional absorption peak at $1661~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ in the FT-IR spectrum of Beta- ${\mathrm{.C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ -NHCO- ${\mathrm{.C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}$ -COOH corresponds to the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ stretching band, which confirms the functionalization of Beta with organic linkers. The characteristic peaks of Beta and UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ in the FTIR spectrum of Beta( $10\%$ -UiO-66-NH2 confirm the presence of both the frameworks in the composite (Fig. 4a). The absorption peaks at 1504 and $1571~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ in the FT-IR spectrum of UiO-66 correspond to the asymmetric stretching vibration of carboxylate linker, whereas, the peak at $1387~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ corresponds to the symmetric stretching vibration of carboxylate linker (Fig. S7).
Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) was carried out to calculate the amount of individual frameworks in the composite material. Thermograms of the composite material and its parent materials are shown in Fig. 4b. The first weight loss in Beta and Beta- ${\mathrm{.C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ -NHCO$\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ -COOH corresponds to the removal of physisorbed $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules. Further, the weight loss in Beta- ${\mathrm{.C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ -NHCO- ${\mathrm{.C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}$ -COOH is related to functionalized linker ((oxobutanoate (CO$\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ -COOH groups) and propylamine (- ${\cdot}{\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ -NH)) present on the surface of Beta (Fig. 4b). The weight loss at $1100\mathrm{K}$ in Beta- ${\mathrm{.C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ -NHCO- ${\mathrm{.C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}$ -COOH is due to the removal of organic species trapped inside the pores of zeolite during functionalization. The weight loss between Beta and Beta- ${\mathrm{.C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ -NHCO- ${\cdot}{\mathrm{C}}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ -COOH corresponds to the amount of functionalized linker anchored to the surface of Beta (Weight loss for Beta- ${\mathrm{.C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ -NHCO- ${\cdot}{\mathrm{C}}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ -COOH, $9.6~\mathrm{wt\%}$ ). Three different weight losses are observed in the TGA profile of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . The first (between $298\mathrm{K}$ and $380\mathrm{K}$ ) and second (between $298\mathrm{K}$ and $380\mathrm{K}$ ) weight losses correspond to the removal of physisorbed $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and occluded solvent molecules, respectively. The final weight loss corresponds to the decomposition of the framework (between $620\mathrm{K}$ and $890~\mathrm{K}$ ). The difference in the weight losses between UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ and Beta $10\%$ -UiO-66-NH2 corresponds to the amount of Beta integrated with MOF $(10.1~\mathrm{wt\%})$ in the composite material (Table 1). Thermogram of UiO-66 is very similar to that of UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ (Fig. S7b).
The chemical state of the various elements present in the highly active Beta $(10\%)$ -UiO$66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ catalyst was investigated by the X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) (Fig. 5). The surface profile confirms the existence of Si, Al, Zr, N, O, and C elements (Fig. 5) in the Beta( $10\%$ -UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ [70]. Fig. 5b shows the core-level XPS spectrum of $\mathrm{Zr~}3\mathrm{d}$ which exhibits two peaks located at $183.6\mathrm{eV}$ and $181.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ that correspond to $\mathrm{Zr}3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ and $\mathrm{Zr}3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ , respectively. The core level C 1s spectrum exhibits three peaks at $287.2\ \mathrm{eV},284.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ ,  and $283.1\mathrm{eV}$ which correspond to $0.0{=}0$ , C-N and C-C of the organic linker ( $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC) of UiO$66\substack{-\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ (Fig. 5c). One peak is obtained for N 1s, which corresponds to $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ group in the composite framework (Fig. 5d). Si 2p (103.3) and Al 2p (76.2) peaks further confirm the integration of Beta with UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ in the composite material (Fig. 5a, inset).
## 3.2. Catalytic activity
The objective of this study is to synthesize 2-((5-(hydroxymethyl)furan-2- yl)methylene)malononitrile by the direct conversion of sucrose via one-pot cascade protocol. This requires a multi-functional catalyst because several consecutive reactions need to take place to afford the final product. In the first step, sucrose hydrolysis produces glucose and fructose. Such hydrolysis is facilitated in the presence of acidic sites (especially Brönsted acidity). Glucose isomerisation provides fructose, which is facilitated in the presence of Lewis acid or Brönsted base [71, 72]. Dehydration of fructose produces HMF. Finally, HMF is converted to 2-((5-(hydroxymethyl)furan-2-yl)methylene)malononitrile in the presence of basic sites present in the multi-functional catalyst. This direct conversion process can be divided into two straight forward reaction steps, i.e., the transformation of sucrose to HMF and then HMF to 2-((5-(hydroxymethyl)furan-2-yl)methylene)malononitrile. It is essential to optimize these two steps separately before carrying out the final one-pot cascade reaction.
Table 2.  Sucrose to HMF transformation by using different catalysts prepared in this study.
The sucrose to HMF conversion process was investigated using Beta, MOFs and zeolite-MOF composite prepared in this study. In the absence of catalyst, no sucrose conversion took place (Table 2, entry 1). However, very low sucrose conversion and HMF selectivity were obtained using UiO-66 (Table 2, entry 2). Low sucrose conversion was due to the low acidity of the catalyst which was unable to catalyze the hydrolysis and subsequent glucose to fructose isomerization steps. $^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR investigation of the reaction mixture revealed that UiO-66 was not stable under the reaction medium and peaks corresponding to the organic linker ( $\mathrm{\cdotH}_{2}\mathrm{BDC})$ were observed in the NMR spectrum of the reaction mixture (Fig. S8). Sucrose conversion was increased from 5 to $30\%$ whereas $80\%$ selectivity for the HMF was observed when UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was used (Table 2, entry 3). This increase in the conversion and selectivity were due to the amine functionalization of Uio-66, which assisted in the glucose conversion to fructose and thus increased the overall reaction yield, especially HMF selectivity. It may be noted that fructose to HMF conversion can take place efficiently in DMSO [14, 16]. Another interesting observation in this reaction was that the organic linker $\mathrm{(H_{2}B D C-N H_{2}}$ ) peak was not observed in the $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectrum of the reaction mixture, which confirmed that UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was stable under the reaction condition.
The structural stability of MOFs was also investigated in DMSO under the reaction temperature for $6\mathrm{{h}}$ . The solution was subjected to $\mathrm{{}^{1}H N M R}$ investigation. NMR investigation showed that under the reaction condition (even in the absence of sucrose), UiO-66 decomposition took place, and the decomposition led to give peaks corresponding to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ (Fig. S9). However, under the similar experimental conditions using UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ , no peak corresponding to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC-NH}_{2}$ was observed (Fig. S10).
Further, the activity of different MOFs was investigated for the Knoevenagel condensation of HMF and malononitrile and the results are summarized in Table 3. Results clearly suggest that amine-functionalized MOF $\mathrm{(UiO-}66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{)}$ was the most suited for this reaction and produced the desired product with $99\%$ yield (Table 3, entry 3). No other product was observed in this catalytic transformation. This study clearly shows that for the second step of the cascade reaction, i.e. Knoevenagel condensation of HMF and malononitrile, UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ is the best catalyst. However, UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ exhibits low activity for the first step of the cascade reaction, i.e. sucrose to HMF transformation. Therefore, for one-pot cascade reaction, it is important to improve the activity of UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ by incorporating some acid sites, which can facilitate the sucrose to HMF transformation.
Table 3.  Knoevenagel condensation of HMF with malononitrile using different catalysts prepared in this study.
These results encouraged us to develop highly sustainable inorganic zeolite Beta and UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ based nanocomposite material. Our group has recently reported that Beta has exhibited good activity for sucrose to HMF conversion [14, 16]. Moreover, the current study reveals that UiO- ${\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ exhibits good activity in the second step of the cascade reaction.
Therefore Beta zeolite was integrated with UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ to achieve multi-functional catalyst Beta-UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . However, a very small amount of Beta $(10.1~\mathrm{wt\%})$ was integrated with UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ to achieve the desired activity. Beta( $10\%$ -UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ was investigated for both the independent steps. Beta $(12\mathrm{mg},10\mathrm{wt}\%)$ afforded low sucrose conversion $(41\%)$ with 83 $\%$ HMF selectivity. In this case glucose, fructose, and formic acid were observed in low selectivity (Table 2, entry 4). Whereas Beta( $10\%$ -UiO-66-NH2 exhibited very high sucrose conversion $(98\%)$ and HMF selectivity $(100~\%)$ . In this case, no other side product was observed. Further, the influence of zeolite Beta loading in the composite material was investigated. With the increase in Beta loading from $2.5\%$ to $12.5\mathrm{wt\%}$ , the sucrose conversion and HMF selectivity were increased (Table 2, entry 5-8). However, with further increase in Beta loading, HMF selectivity was slightly reduced (Table 2, entry 8). Therefore $10\mathrm{\mt{\%}}$ of Beta loading was optimized for the first step of the cascade reaction. The influence of temperature was also investigated (Fig. S11) and obtained result shows that with an increase in the temperature from $363~\mathrm{K}$ to $393\mathrm{~K~}$ , the conversion of sucrose was increased from $34\%$ to $99\%$ . A physical mixture of Beta $(10\%)$ and UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ exhibited less activity (conversion and selectivity) when compared to Beta $10\%$ -UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ (Table 2, entry 9) confirming the usefulness of the developed composite material. Further, $\mathrm{Beta}(10\%)\mathrm{-UiO-}66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was also investigated for the Knoevenagel condensation reaction. In the Knoevenagel condensation reaction between HMF and malononitrile using Beta (12 and $100\mathrm{mg}$ ), very low product yield was obtained which confirmed that acidity of zeolite Beta was not suitable for this reaction (Table 3, entry 4, 5). Beta( $10\%$ -UiO-66-NH2 exhibited excellent activity and produced the desired product in high yield $(96~\%)$ (Table 3, entry 8). In this case also, the influence of Beta loading was investigated. With increase in Beta loading from $2.5\mathrm{wt}\%$ to $10\mathrm{\mt\%}$ , the product yield was marginally reduced from $99\%$ to $96\%$ (Table 3, entries 6-8). A physical mixture of Beta $(10\%)$ and UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ exhibited less activity when compared to Beta $(10\%)$ -UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (Table 3, entry 10) which confirmed that composite material was required to achieve a better yield. The catalyst exhibited almost similar activity after five cycles for Knoevenagel condensation reaction (Table 3, entry 11). Furthermore, the recycling experiments were also performed at the intermediate stage (after $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ ) and the obtained results showed that the activity of Beta $10\%$ -UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ remained consistent even after five cycles (Fig. S12). The influence of solvent in the Knoevenagel condensation was also investigated (Table S1). The reaction proceeded efficiently in the neat condition (Table S1). Among the polar solvents, DMSO exhibited the best result. Non polar solvent such as toluene was not as good as the polar solvents (Table S1).
Considering both steps of cascade reaction, Beta $(10\%)–\mathrm{UiO}–66–\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was finally developed for the one-pot two-steps cascade reaction for the formation of 2-((5- (hydroxymethyl)furan-2-yl)methylene)malononitrile from sucrose (Scheme 1b). First sucrose (1 mmol) was heated at $393\mathrm{~K~}$ for $6\textup{h}$ under a stirring condition in DMSO ( $5~\mathrm{mL}$ ) in the presence of $\mathrm{Beta}(10\%)\mathrm{-UiO-}66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ ( $\mathrm{120~mg)}$ . During this time, sucrose was completely converted to HMF (As confirmed from $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR). Then reaction temperature was reduced to $373\mathrm{K}$ and malononitrile $(2.5\mathrm{mmol})$ ) was added and the reaction was continued for $1.5\mathrm{h}$ under the stirring condition to achieve the high yield of the desired product ( $\mathrm{^{IH}}$ NMR conversion (based on sucrose) $=96\%$ )) (Fig. 6 and Table S2).
Having achieved the success with sucrose, fructose (1 mmol) was heated at $393\mathrm{~K~}$ for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ in the absence of catalyst, which led to the complete conversion of fructose to HMF. Then the reaction temperature was reduced to $373\mathrm{~K~}$ , malononitrile ( $\mathrm{1.25~mmol)}$ and Beta $(10\%)$ - $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ ( $\mathrm{100~mg)}$ were added and the reaction was continued for $1.5\mathrm{~h~}$ under stirring condition to achieve the high yield of the desired product (IH NMR conversion (based on fructose) $=99\%$ )) (Fig. S13 and Table S2).
When the similar reaction was conducted with glucose (1 mmol) at $393\mathrm{~K~}$ for $10\mathrm{~h~}$ in the presence of Beta $(10\%)$ -UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ ( $\mathrm{120mg)}$ , glucose was completely converted to HMF. Then the reaction temperature was reduced to $373\mathrm{K}$ , and malononitrile ( $1.25\mathrm{mmol}\$ ) was added and the reaction was continued for $1.5\mathrm{h}$ under the stirring condition to achieve the high yield of the desired product ( $\mathrm{^{IH}}$ NMR conversion (based on glucose) $=98\%$ ) (Fig. 7 and Table S2).
In carbohydrate conversion, the yield determined from $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR of the reaction mixture is not always accurate; therefore it was important to isolate the product and determine the isolated product yield. To isolate the final compound, the reaction mixture was extracted with MIBK (three times). The reaction mixture was evaporated to dryness and then subjected to column chromatography with silica gel (100-200 mesh) and solvent (ethyl acetate: hexane $\mathbf{\Sigma}=$ 1:1 (V/V)) to get the pure product. The purity of the isolated product was also confirmed from $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR (Fig. S14, S15). During the extraction process, some amount of humin (Fig. S16) was obtained, which was not observed in the $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectra of the reaction mixture. Therefore, the isolated yields in all the cases for the final product were different when compared to the yield obtained using $\mathrm{{}^{1}H\ N M R}$ given above. The isolated yields for the final product 2-((5- (hydroxymethyl)furan-2-yl)methylene)malononitrile obtained by the one-pot cascade reaction with fructose, sucrose, and glucose were $72\%$ , $65\%$ , and $60\%$ , respectively. It may be noted that the second step (Knoevenagel condensation) took $0.5\mathrm{~h~}$ for the complete conversion of HMF to 2-((5-(hydroxymethyl)furan-2-yl)methylene)malononitrile. However, in the one-pot cascade process, it took $1.5\mathrm{h}$ for the same reaction step. This can be correlated to the humin present in the reaction medium. Humin is known to block the active sites of the catalyst. It is reported in the literature that the catalyst exhibited deactivation phenomenon due to the deposition of humin on the catalyst surface [21, 27]. Based on the time duration taken for the second step in the one-pot cascade reaction, one can say that the humin influenced the reaction by enveloping some of the active sites. However, in the present study, no deactivation of the catalyst took place. The catalyst exhibited only a marginal loss in the activity after five cycles in the one-pot cascade reaction (Fig. S17). Isolated yields for 2-((5-(hydroxymethyl)furan-2- yl)methylene)malononitrile were $65\%$ , $63.5\%$ , $61.9\%$ , $60.8\%$ , and $60.3\%$ after the $1^{\mathrm{st}},2^{\mathrm{nd}},3^{\mathrm{rd}};$ $4^{\mathrm{th}}$ and $5^{\mathrm{th}}$ recycles, respectively. XRD (Fig. S18a), FT-IR (Fig. S18b), TGA (Fig. S18c), and textural properties (Table 1) of the recycled catalyst confirm that the catalyst was stable after the recycling experiments. No broadening in the XRD pattern was observed for the recovered catalyst. Furthermore, no additional peak was observed in the XRD pattern of the recovered catalyst, which has been observed after the humin formation in some previously reported catalysts (Fig. S18a) [27]. This observation confirms that washing with water, MIBK, and ethanol leads to the removal of deposited humin, if any, on the catalyst surface. Therefore, it provided consistent activity during its recycling experiments. Moreover, Beta-UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ was solvent extracted for $6\mathrm{~h~}$ using ethanol and water, and XRD patterns of the obtained materials were recorded. No changes in the XRD patterns were obtained confirming the stability of the catalyst in various solvents (Fig. S19).
Existence of $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ sites in Beta-UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ can also be confirmed from zeta potential. Zeta potential was measured in DMSO because the reaction medium for the cascade reaction was DMSO. Beta-UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ exhibited a zeta potential of $15.7~\mathrm{mV}$ and confirmed the existence of cationic species (labile $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ (Brönsted acidity)) in the material. Literature report suggests that with increase in the hydrophilicity of surface, zeta potential increases [73]. Upon addition of sucrose in the DMSO, zeta potential was increased to $17.9~\mathrm{mV}$ from $15.7~\mathrm{mV}$ . When sucrose was adsorbed on the catalyst surface, it increased the hydrophilicity of the surface due to the presence of large number of $-\mathrm{OH}$ functionality in the adsorbed sucrose molecule. Further, upon addition of the HMF to the DMSO medium, zeta potential was decreased to $8.8~\mathrm{\mV}$ . HMF was adsorbed on the catalyst surface and decreased the hydrophilicity of the surface which decreased the zeta potential. Zeta potential was further decreased to $8.2\mathrm{mV}$ when malononitrile was added to the above solution confirming a further decrease in the hydrophilicity of the surface by the adsorption of malononitrile (Fig. S20).
Based on the obtained results, a mechanism is proposed (Scheme S1). Beta-UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ acts as a multi-functional catalyst. The $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ sites of Beta and $\mathrm{Zr^{4+}}/\mathrm{Al^{3+}}$ sites present in the $\mathrm{Beta}(10\%)\mathrm{-UiO-}66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ provide required acidity (strength and type (Brönsted and Lewis) for the hydrolysis followed by isomerization leading to HMF with the assistance of DMSO (DMSO facilitates the dehydration step). Moreover, $\left.\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\right.$ group in the framework also assists in the isomerization of glucose to fructose) [70]. The second step (the Knoevenagel condensation) of the cascade reaction is facilitated by the bi-functional sites, especially, Lewis acidity and Brönsted basicity $\left(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ .
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, zeolite Beta-MOF nanocomposite was successfully prepared for the one-pot, twosteps cascade reaction for the synthesis of carbohydrate derived 2-((5-(hydroxymethyl)furan2-yl)methylene)malononitrile. TGA analysis confirmed that $10.1~\mathrm{wt\%}$ Beta was incorporated in the composite catalyst. Functional groups and elemental analysis results derived from FTIR and XPS analysis, respectively, confirmed the formation of integrated Beta-UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ nanocomposites. TEM investigation confirmed that $60{-}80\mathrm{nm}\mathrm{UiO{-}}66{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{NH_{2}}$ was integrated with 10-20 nm zeolite Beta particles. Beta( $10\%$ -UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ exhibited excellent activity in both, sucrose to HMF and Knoevenagel condensation reactions. Moreover, Beta( $10\%$ -UiO-66-NH2 exhibited very good activity in the direct one-pot cascade conversion of sucrose and fructose to 2-((5-(hydroxymethyl)furan-2-yl)methylene)malononitrile with isolated yields of $65\%$ and
$72\%$ , respectively. Interestingly, Beta( $10\%$ )-UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ also exhibited good activity for the glucose, in the direct conversion to 2-((5-(hydroxymethyl)furan-2-yl)methylene)malononitrile with an isolated yield of $60\%$ . Multi-functional sites present in Beta( $10\%$ )-UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ was key for the success of one-pot cascade reaction. Brönsted acid sites of Beta( $10\%$ )-UiO-66-NH2 facilitated the hydrolysis of sucrose to glucose and fructose, Lewis acid sites (Al and $Z\mathrm{r}$ sites) and functional $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ present in $\mathrm{Beta}(10\%)\mathrm{-UiO-}66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ facilitated the isomerization of glucose to fructose, and  Lewis acid sites (Al a Zr sites) & functional $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ of Beta( $10\%$ -UiO$66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ were required for the conversion of HMF to 2-((5-(hydroxymethyl)furan-2- yl)methylene)malononitrile. The developed nanocomposite was stable and recyclable during the one-pot cascade reaction. Multi-functional catalysts have been reported for the one-pot cascade reaction for the carbohydrate conversion to 2,5-furandicaboxyldehyde and 2,5- furandicarboxyxlic acid, whereas in the present study a novel Beta( $10\%$ -UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ has been developed for the one-pot cascade conversion of carbohydrate to another specialty chemical 2- ((5-(hydroxymethyl)furan-2-yl)methylene)malononitrile. This study provides a unique direction for the development of multi-functional catalyst by the integration of a wide range of inorganic zeolites and MOFs depending on the catalytic activity of MOFs and zeolites for the desired catalytic reactions towards the synthesis of specialty chemicals from renewable biomass.
